From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: server, advapi32, secur32: a domain account's real SID

A domain user signed in to a member machine had a SID of this machine's
(S-1-5-21-0-0-0-<1000 + uid>, patch 0002): their token, the files they
create, their HKCU hive and every ACL named an account no other machine in
the domain knows, and LookupAccountSid/Name had no domain names at all.

sg-session's sg-domain-logon asks winbind at sign-in, as root and before
the session's first Windows program, and writes
/run/stained-glass/domain-sids/<uid>: the account's SID and DOMAIN\name,
its primary group and domain groups ("group"), and the domain's
well-known groups it is not in ("name", for ACLs). The server believes the
file only when it and its directory are root's and writable by no one
else; it never calls winbind itself (a lookup can block).

- The uid's SID is the domain SID: tokens, file owners, the HKCU path
  (the hive file is per uid, so settings carry over). A uid whose hive is
  already loaded under the local SID keeps it until the server restarts
  rather than losing HKCU; once a uid has its domain SID it keeps it.
- Tokens also hold the domain groups; the first (primaryGroupID, normally
  Domain Users) is the primary group, so it is a new file's group. Local
  groups stay (this machine's descriptors name them); Administrators is
  still only SYSTEM's -- a Domain Admin elevates through the broker.
- sg_lookup_account (0017) answers domain accounts and groups without a
  protocol change: rid 0 and a SID string gives "DOMAIN\name", rid 0 and a
  name ("DOMAIN\name" or "name") gives "<SID>\t<DOMAIN>", with rid
  0x80000001 (user) or 0x80000002 (group). advapi32's LookupAccountSid and
  LookupAccountName (and the current user's own domain) use it;
  GetUserNameEx(NameSamCompatible) is DOMAIN\name.

